{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And Elohim tested Abraham.</b> The concept of testing, in my opinion, is that since a person's deed is absolute free will in his hand — if he wishes he does, and if he does not wish he does not — it is called a \"test\" from the perspective of the one being tested. But the Tester, blessed be He, commands in it to bring the matter from potentiality into actuality, so that there may be reward for a good deed, not reward for a good heart alone. Know that HaShem tests the righteous (Psalms 11:5): when He knows concerning a righteous person that he will do His will, and He desires to vindicate him, He commands him in a test, and He does not test the wicked, who will not obey. Behold, all the tests in the Torah are for the benefit of the one being tested."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {0 "Ramban cites Psalms 11:5 from the phrase יהוה צדיק יבחן; I have rendered HaShem for יהוה per the transliteration rule, and standardized the verse citation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:33:01.920055397Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}